{"reviewer_participant_id": "actor:dependence-free-batch-c-reviewer-fable-20", "reviews": [{"case_id": "case:d674ebb8c31ed83be287", "verdict": "demonstrated_issue", "issue_class": "issue-class:repeated-authorized-independent-unit-entry-into-row-independent-procedure", "bounded_statement": "Within the supplied files for this case, the workflow feeds all 40 plot-level rows (four rows per terrace, ten terraces) into a Welch two-sample t-test that treats every row as an independent observation, even though the data description designates terrace_id as the independent-unit column and treatment was assigned at whole-terrace level. The reported selected result (t = 7.44, df = 35.31, p < 0.001, n = 40) therefore rests on repeated entries of each independent unit into a row-independent procedure, with only 5 treated and 5 untreated independent units actually present. This statement is bounded to the stated statistical conclusion; it does not claim the direction of effect is wrong (a terrace-level aggregation of the same data would still show biochar terraces higher, at roughly t ≈ 3.75 with df ≈ 8, but with materially weaker evidence than reported).", "root_cause": "The analysis script reads each CSV row as one observation (read_plots/rates in workflow/analysis.py) and passes the 20 + 20 plot-level values directly to scipy.stats.ttest_ind with no per-terrace aggregation or clustering adjustment, despite the treatment being applied to whole terraces and terrace_id being the declared independent unit. The four within-terrace readings are pseudo-replicates of the same experimental unit, so the test's sample sizes (20 per group) and degrees of freedom (35.31) overstate the true independent replication (5 terraces per group, df ≈ 8), inflating the strength of evidence reported.", "evidence_atoms": [{"description": "The data description explicitly names terrace_id as the independent-unit column and states each terrace contributes four rows, establishing that rows are not independent units.", "observed_value": "Independent unit column: terrace_id; four rows per terrace", "source_spans": [{"path": "data-description.md", "start_line": 22, "end_line": 23, "quoted_text": "One row is: one double-ring infiltrometer reading taken at one marked sampling plot on one terrace\nIndependent unit column: terrace_id"}, {"path": "data-description.md", "start_line": 6, "end_line": 8, "quoted_text": "On every terrace the survey crew measured steady-state infiltration\nwith a double-ring infiltrometer at four permanently marked sampling plots, so\neach terrace contributes four rows to the file."}]}, {"description": "Treatment is assigned at terrace level, so all four plot rows within a terrace share the same treatment and are not independent replicates of the treatment contrast.", "observed_value": "treatment identical for all four plots on a terrace", "source_spans": [{"path": "data-description.md", "start_line": 14, "end_line": 16, "quoted_text": "- `treatment` - `biochar` if that terrace received the topsoil amendment,\n  `none` if it did not. The treatment was applied to whole terraces, so this\n  value is the same for all four plots on a terrace."}]}, {"description": "The workflow passes plot-level lists (20 values per group, four per terrace) straight into a row-independent two-sample t-test with no per-terrace aggregation, clustering, or random effect.", "observed_value": "stats.ttest_ind(amended, unamended, equal_var=False) on 40 plot rows", "source_spans": [{"path": "workflow/analysis.py", "start_line": 44, "end_line": 54, "quoted_text": "def main():\n    plots = read_plots(INPUT_PATH)\n    n_total = len(plots)\n\n    amended = rates(plots, AMENDED)\n    unamended = rates(plots, UNAMENDED)\n\n    n_a, mean_a, sd_a = summarise(amended)\n    n_u, mean_u, sd_u = summarise(unamended)\n\n    outcome = stats.ttest_ind(amended, unamended, equal_var=False)"}, {"path": "workflow/analysis.py", "start_line": 27, "end_line": 28, "quoted_text": "def rates(plots, label):\n    return [float(plot[RATE_COLUMN]) for plot in plots if plot[GROUP_COLUMN] == label]"}]}, {"description": "The report's stated analysis and selected result confirm every one of the 40 plot rows was entered as an independent observation, yielding n = 20 per group and df = 35.31 instead of the 5 independent terraces per group (df ≈ 8).", "observed_value": "n = 40 rows, t = 7.44, df = 35.31, p < 0.001", "source_spans": [{"path": "results/report.md", "start_line": 20, "end_line": 22, "quoted_text": "Each of the 40 plot measurements was entered as one observation and the two\ntreatment groups were compared with a Welch two-sample t-test\n(`scipy.stats.ttest_ind`, `equal_var=False`), two-sided."}, {"path": "results/report.md", "start_line": 30, "end_line": 30, "quoted_text": "[selected-result] Welch two-sample t-test on 40 plot infiltration measurements: biochar-amended plots averaged 13.00 mm/h higher than unamended plots (63.00 vs 50.00 mm/h), t = 7.44, df = 35.31, p < 0.001."}]}, {"description": "The input data confirm four rows per terrace_id with identical treatment and strongly clustered values within terrace (e.g., TR-01 spans 52-58 while TR-05 spans 68-74), so within-terrace rows are not interchangeable with between-terrace rows.", "observed_value": "TR-01: 52, 54, 56, 58 (all biochar); TR-05: 68, 70, 72, 74 (all biochar)", "source_spans": [{"path": "data/input.csv", "start_line": 2, "end_line": 5, "quoted_text": "TR-01,biochar,P1,52\nTR-01,biochar,P2,54\nTR-01,biochar,P3,56\nTR-01,biochar,P4,58"}, {"path": "data/input.csv", "start_line": 18, "end_line": 21, "quoted_text": "TR-05,biochar,P1,68\nTR-05,biochar,P2,70\nTR-05,biochar,P3,72\nTR-05,biochar,P4,74"}]}], "counterevidence_atoms": [{"description": "The report is transparent about the design and the procedure: it discloses that measurements come from four plots on each of ten terraces and that all 40 plot rows were entered as observations. This openness argues against concealment, but it does not change the fact that the row-independent test received repeated entries per independent unit.", "observed_value": "Report discloses 4 plots per terrace and states each plot measurement was one observation", "source_spans": [{"path": "results/report.md", "start_line": 5, "end_line": 9, "quoted_text": "Ten restored hillside terraces in the Kalu catchment were surveyed after two wet\nseasons. Five terraces had received a biochar topsoil amendment; five received\nnone. Field crews recorded steady-state infiltration with a double-ring\ninfiltrometer at four sampling plots on every terrace. This report asks whether\namended plots infiltrate faster than unamended plots."}]}, {"description": "The report frames its question at plot level ('whether amended plots infiltrate faster than unamended plots'), which could be read as intentionally choosing plots as the analysis unit. However, treatment was assigned at terrace level and the description designates terrace_id as the independent unit, so a plot-level framing cannot make the 40 rows independent for the treatment comparison.", "observed_value": "Plot-level framing of the research question", "source_spans": [{"path": "results/report.md", "start_line": 8, "end_line": 9, "quoted_text": "infiltrometer at four sampling plots on every terrace. This report asks whether\namended plots infiltrate faster than unamended plots."}, {"path": "data-description.md", "start_line": 23, "end_line": 23, "quoted_text": "Independent unit column: terrace_id"}]}], "falsification_attempt": "I attempted to falsify the issue in three ways. First, I searched workflow/analysis.py for any per-terrace aggregation, group-by on terrace_id, clustering correction, or mixed/random-effect structure that would restore one entry per independent unit before the test; there is none — terrace_id is never referenced in the script, and the rates() function returns all plot rows per treatment directly into stats.ttest_ind. Second, I checked whether the reported n and df could be consistent with a terrace-level analysis: the report states n = 20 per group and df = 35.31, which matches plot-level entry (4 × 5 terraces per group), not terrace-level entry (which would give n = 5 per group and df ≈ 8). Third, I considered whether rows might legitimately be independent despite the description (e.g., if treatment varied within terrace); the description and the CSV both show treatment constant within terrace and values strongly clustered by terrace, ruling this out. As a robustness check I recomputed a terrace-level Welch t-test from terrace means (biochar: 55, 59, 63, 67, 71; none: 44, 48, 50, 52, 56), giving t ≈ 3.75 on df ≈ 8 with p ≈ 0.006 — the direction survives, but the reported t = 7.44, df = 35.31 materially overstate the evidence, confirming the issue affects the stated statistical conclusion rather than being harmless.", "cross_case_evidence_used": false, "unresolved_material_questions": [], "self_reported_confidence": "high"}]}